\chapter{A complete worked analysis}

This chapter runs the whole workflow end to end on one dataset. It is simulated,
deliberately: because we know the true answer, you can see at each step whether the
method is recovering it or being fooled.

\section{The scenario}

A health programme is evaluated across 40 clinics. Each clinic enrols patients, and
for each patient we record the number of malaria episodes in a follow-up period.
Key features, all of which are typical of real data:

\begin{itemize}
  \item follow-up time differs between patients $\Rightarrow$ we need an
        \textbf{offset};
  \item patients are nested in clinics $\Rightarrow$ we need a \textbf{random
        effect};
  \item patients differ beyond what we measured $\Rightarrow$
        \textbf{overdispersion};
  \item the treatment is assigned \textbf{at clinic level}, which is where
        ignoring clustering does the most damage.
\end{itemize}

<<sim-data>>=
set.seed(2025)
n_clinic  <- 40
n_per     <- 40
clinic    <- rep(1:n_clinic, each = n_per)
treated   <- rep(rbinom(n_clinic, 1, 0.5), each = n_per)   # assigned per CLINIC
clinic_re <- rep(rnorm(n_clinic, 0, 0.45), each = n_per)   # clinic variation
age       <- round(runif(n_clinic * n_per, 1, 60))
bednet    <- rbinom(n_clinic * n_per, 1, 0.4)
fu_years  <- round(runif(n_clinic * n_per, 0.25, 2.0), 2)  # unequal follow-up

## TRUE model: log rate = 0.2 - 0.45*treated - 0.012*age - 0.35*bednet + clinic RE
log_rate  <- 0.2 - 0.45 * treated - 0.012 * age - 0.35 * bednet + clinic_re
mu        <- exp(log_rate + log(fu_years))
episodes  <- rnbinom(length(mu), mu = mu, size = 2.5)      # overdispersed

mal <- data.frame(
  episodes, fu_years, clinic = factor(clinic),
  treated = factor(treated, 0:1, c("control", "programme")),
  age, bednet = factor(bednet, 0:1, c("no", "yes"))
)
str(mal)
@

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Quantity} & \textbf{True value} \\
\midrule
Programme rate ratio & $e^{-0.45} = \Sexpr{round(exp(-0.45), 3)}$ \\
Bednet rate ratio    & $e^{-0.35} = \Sexpr{round(exp(-0.35), 3)}$ \\
Rate ratio per year of age & $e^{-0.012} = \Sexpr{round(exp(-0.012), 4)}$ \\
Clinic SD (log scale) & $0.45$ \\
Dispersion $\theta$   & $2.5$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Step 1: look at the data}

<<eda, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=6.2, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Exploratory plots. Note the long right tail, the very different follow-up times, and the visible spread between clinic-level rates -- each of which points at a specific modelling decision.">>=
par(mfrow = c(2, 2)); par_book()
barplot(table(factor(mal$episodes, levels = 0:max(mal$episodes))),
        col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.7), border = "white",
        xlab = "malaria episodes", ylab = "patients", main = "Outcome distribution")

hist(mal$fu_years, breaks = 20, col = adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.7),
     border = "white", xlab = "follow-up (years)", main = "Exposure varies 8-fold")

rate_by_clinic <- tapply(mal$episodes, mal$clinic, sum) /
                  tapply(mal$fu_years, mal$clinic, sum)
trt_by_clinic  <- tapply(as.integer(mal$treated), mal$clinic, mean)
stripchart(rate_by_clinic ~ ifelse(trt_by_clinic > 1.5, "programme", "control"),
           vertical = TRUE, method = "jitter", pch = 21,
           bg = adjustcolor(PAL["green"], 0.6), col = "white", cex = 1.3,
           ylab = "clinic episode rate per year", main = "Clinic-level rates")
grid_book()

plot(mal$age, mal$episodes / mal$fu_years, pch = 21,
     bg = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.35), col = "white", cex = 0.8,
     xlab = "age (years)", ylab = "crude rate per year", main = "Rate against age")
grid_book()
lines(lowess(mal$age, mal$episodes / mal$fu_years), lwd = 3, col = PAL["red"])
@

<<eda-numbers>>=
c(n_patients = nrow(mal), n_clinics = nlevels(mal$clinic),
  mean_episodes = round(mean(mal$episodes), 2),
  var_episodes = round(var(mal$episodes), 2),
  percent_zero = round(100 * mean(mal$episodes == 0), 1))
@

Variance is already about
\Sexpr{round(var(mal$episodes)/mean(mal$episodes), 1)} times the mean in the raw
data. That is a hint, not proof --- some of that spread will be explained by the
predictors, and the rest is what the dispersion check in Step 4 has to judge.

\section{Step 2: the naive model (what not to do)}

<<naive-model>>=
m0 <- glm(episodes ~ treated + age + bednet, family = poisson, data = mal)
round(exp(cbind(IRR = coef(m0), confint.default(m0))), 4)
@

Two errors are already baked in: no offset, so patients followed longer look sicker;
and no clustering, so the intervals are far too narrow. The programme rate ratio is
\Sexpr{round(exp(coef(m0))["treatedprogramme"], 3)} against a truth of
\Sexpr{round(exp(-0.45), 3)}.

\section{Step 3: add the offset}

<<offset-model>>=
m1 <- glm(episodes ~ treated + age + bednet + offset(log(fu_years)),
          family = poisson, data = mal)
round(exp(cbind(IRR = coef(m1), confint.default(m1))), 4)
@

Much better --- the programme estimate is now
\Sexpr{round(exp(coef(m1))["treatedprogramme"], 3)}, close to the true
\Sexpr{round(exp(-0.45), 3)}.

\section{Step 4: check dispersion}

<<disp-check>>=
disp <- function(m) {
  X2 <- sum(residuals(m, type = "pearson")^2); df <- df.residual(m)
  c(dispersion = X2 / df, p = pchisq(X2, df, lower.tail = FALSE))
}
round(disp(m1), 4)
AER::dispersiontest(m1, trafo = 2)
@

Dispersion is \Sexpr{round(disp(m1)["dispersion"], 2)}, decisively above 1. The
standard errors from \fn{m1} are too small by roughly
$\sqrt{\Sexpr{round(disp(m1)["dispersion"], 2)}} =
\Sexpr{round(sqrt(disp(m1)["dispersion"]), 2)}$.

Before blaming the distribution, check the mean model (Chapter 7's advice):

<<check-shape, fig.width=7, fig.height=3.8, out.width="0.8\\textwidth", fig.cap="Quantile residuals against age. Flat, so a linear term for age is adequate and the overdispersion is not caused by a missing curve.">>=
set.seed(3)
qres <- function(y, pfun, ...) {
  a <- pfun(y - 1, ...); b <- pfun(y, ...)
  qnorm(pmin(pmax(runif(length(y), pmin(a,b), pmax(a,b)), 1e-10), 1 - 1e-10))
}
par_book()
qr1 <- qres(mal$episodes, ppois, lambda = fitted(m1))
plot(mal$age, qr1, pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.3), col = "white",
     cex = 0.9, xlab = "age", ylab = "quantile residual",
     main = "Is the effect of age linear on the log scale?")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, col = PAL["grey"], lwd = 1.5)
lines(lowess(mal$age, qr1), lwd = 3, col = PAL["red"])
@

<<check-nonlinear>>=
m1_spline <- glm(episodes ~ treated + splines::ns(age, 4) + bednet +
                 offset(log(fu_years)), family = poisson, data = mal)
anova(m1, m1_spline, test = "LRT")
@

No evidence of curvature. The overdispersion is real, not a misspecified mean.

\section{Step 5: negative binomial}

<<nb-step>>=
m2 <- MASS::glm.nb(episodes ~ treated + age + bednet + offset(log(fu_years)),
                   data = mal)
c(theta = round(m2$theta, 3), true_theta = 2.5)
round(c(AIC_poisson = AIC(m1), AIC_negbin = AIC(m2)), 1)
pscl::odTest(m2)
@

$\hat\theta = \Sexpr{round(m2$theta, 2)}$, noticeably \emph{smaller} than the true
value of 2.5 --- and a smaller $\theta$ means more dispersion. That is not a
mistake, it is a symptom. This model has only one place to put extra variability,
so it is absorbing the patient-level heterogeneity we built in \emph{and} the
clinic-level variation it has no way to represent. Step 6 separates the two, and
$\hat\theta$ moves back towards its true value once it does.

\section{Step 6: add the random effect}

<<glmm-step>>=
library(glmmTMB)
m3 <- glmmTMB(episodes ~ treated + age + bednet + offset(log(fu_years)) +
              (1 | clinic), family = nbinom2, data = mal)
summary(m3)
@

<<glmm-varcomp>>=
sd_clinic <- sqrt(as.numeric(VarCorr(m3)$cond$clinic))
c(estimated_clinic_sd = round(sd_clinic, 3), true_clinic_sd = 0.45,
  estimated_theta = round(sigma(m3), 3), true_theta = 2.5)
@

Both the clinic standard deviation and $\theta$ are now close to their true values.
Splitting the variation into ``between clinics'' and ``between patients'' has let
each parameter find its own level.

\section{Step 7: compare everything}

<<compare-all>>=
truth <- c(treatedprogramme = -0.45, age = -0.012, bednetyes = -0.35)
get <- function(m, nm) {
  cf <- if (inherits(m, "glmmTMB")) fixef(m)$cond else coef(m)
  se <- if (inherits(m, "glmmTMB")) sqrt(diag(vcov(m)$cond))
        else sqrt(diag(vcov(m)))
  c(est = cf[nm], se = se[nm])
}
tab <- t(sapply(list(
  "1. Poisson, no offset"     = m0,
  "2. Poisson + offset"       = m1,
  "3. Negative binomial"      = m2,
  "4. NB + clinic random eff" = m3
), get, nm = "treatedprogramme"))
out <- data.frame(
  IRR       = round(exp(tab[, 1]), 3),
  lower     = round(exp(tab[, 1] - 1.96 * tab[, 2]), 3),
  upper     = round(exp(tab[, 1] + 1.96 * tab[, 2]), 3),
  CI_width  = round(exp(tab[, 1] + 1.96*tab[, 2]) - exp(tab[, 1] - 1.96*tab[, 2]), 3)
)
out$covers_truth <- out$lower <= exp(-0.45) & out$upper >= exp(-0.45)
out
@

<<compare-plot, fig.width=7.6, fig.height=4, out.width="0.85\\textwidth", fig.cap="The programme effect under four models. The truth is the dashed line. Each fix widens the interval; only the last model both centres on the truth and reports honest uncertainty.">>=
par_book()
k <- nrow(out)
plot(out$IRR, k:1, xlim = range(c(out$lower, out$upper, exp(-0.45))) * c(0.95, 1.05),
     ylim = c(0.5, k + 0.5), pch = 19, cex = 1.6, col = PAL["primary"], yaxt = "n",
     xlab = "rate ratio for the programme", ylab = "",
     main = "Each correction widens the interval")
axis(2, at = k:1, labels = rownames(out), las = 1, cex.axis = 0.65)
grid_book()
arrows(out$lower, k:1, out$upper, k:1, code = 3, angle = 90, length = 0.05,
       lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["primary"])
abline(v = exp(-0.45), lwd = 2.5, lty = 2, col = PAL["red"])
abline(v = 1, lwd = 1.5, lty = 3, col = PAL["grey"])
text(exp(-0.45), k + 0.42, "truth", col = PAL["red"], cex = 0.75, pos = 4)
@

\begin{keyidea}[What the table shows]
Model 1 is wrong and confident. Model 2 fixes the point estimate but is still
overconfident. Model 3 widens the interval by accounting for patient heterogeneity.
Model 4 widens it again by accounting for clinic heterogeneity --- and its interval
is roughly
\Sexpr{round(out$CI_width[4] / out$CI_width[2], 1)} times as wide as model 2's.
That extra width is not a loss. It is the honest amount of uncertainty in a study
where treatment was assigned to 40 clinics, not to 1000 patients.
\end{keyidea}

\section{Step 8: check the final model}

<<final-check, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Diagnostics for the final model: quantile residuals lie on the normal line, and simulated datasets reproduce both the number of zeros and the largest observed count.">>=
set.seed(8)
mu_hat <- fitted(m3)
qr3 <- qres(mal$episodes, pnbinom, mu = mu_hat, size = sigma(m3))
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
qqnorm(qr3, pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["green"], 0.4), col = "white", cex = 0.9,
       main = "Quantile residuals: final model")
qqline(qr3, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["primary"]); grid_book()

sims <- simulate(m3, nsim = 300)
p0 <- sapply(sims, function(z) mean(z == 0))
hist(p0, breaks = 25, col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.55), border = "white",
     xlab = "proportion of zeros", main = "Simulated vs observed zeros")
abline(v = mean(mal$episodes == 0), lwd = 3, col = PAL["red"])
@

<<final-check-numbers>>=
round(c(sd_qres = sd(qr3),
        observed_zeros = mean(mal$episodes == 0),
        simulated_zeros_mean = mean(p0),
        simulated_p = mean(p0 >= mean(mal$episodes == 0))), 4)
@

Quantile residuals have standard deviation \Sexpr{round(sd(qr3), 2)}, sit on the
normal line, and the model reproduces the zeros. The model fits.

\section{Step 9: report it}

<<final-report>>=
ci <- confint(m3, parm = "beta_")
report <- data.frame(
  IRR   = round(exp(ci[, "Estimate"]), 3),
  lower = round(exp(ci[, 1]), 3),
  upper = round(exp(ci[, 2]), 3)
)
rownames(report) <- rownames(ci)
report
@

\begin{plainenglish}[The paragraph you would write]
``We modelled the number of malaria episodes per patient with a negative binomial
mixed model, using $\log(\text{follow-up years})$ as an offset and a random
intercept for clinic. Patients in programme clinics had an incidence rate ratio of
\Sexpr{round(exp(ci["treatedprogramme", "Estimate"]), 2)}
(95\% CI \Sexpr{round(exp(ci["treatedprogramme", 1]), 2)}--\Sexpr{round(exp(ci["treatedprogramme", 2]), 2)})
compared with control clinics, adjusting for age and bednet use. Bednet use was
associated with an IRR of
\Sexpr{round(exp(ci["bednetyes", "Estimate"]), 2)}
(95\% CI \Sexpr{round(exp(ci["bednetyes", 1]), 2)}--\Sexpr{round(exp(ci["bednetyes", 2]), 2)}).
Counts were strongly overdispersed under a Poisson model (Pearson dispersion
\Sexpr{round(disp(m1)["dispersion"], 1)}); the fitted dispersion parameter was
$\hat\theta = \Sexpr{round(sigma(m3), 2)}$ and the between-clinic standard
deviation was \Sexpr{round(sd_clinic, 2)} on the log scale.''
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The workflow as a checklist}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily\footnotesize, node distance=4mm,
  b/.style={rectangle, rounded corners=2pt, draw=rule1, fill=white, align=left,
            inner sep=5pt, text width=112mm},
  ar/.style={-{Stealth[length=4pt]}, draw=rule1, thick}]
\node[b, fill=primarylt, draw=primary] (a)
  {\textbf{1. Look at the data.} Distribution of the outcome, proportion of zeros,
   variance vs mean, exposure, cluster sizes.};
\node[b, below=of a] (b)
  {\textbf{2. Identify the structure.} Is there exposure (offset)? Are rows
   clustered? Are predictors at the individual or the group level?};
\node[b, below=of b] (c)
  {\textbf{3. Fit the Poisson with the offset.} This is the reference point.};
\node[b, below=of c] (d)
  {\textbf{4. Check the mean model.} Quantile residuals against every predictor;
   test for curvature before blaming the distribution.};
\node[b, below=of d] (e)
  {\textbf{5. Measure dispersion.} Pearson $X^2$/df, \fn{dispersiontest}, predicted
   vs observed zeros.};
\node[b, below=of e] (f)
  {\textbf{6. Fix what is broken.} Negative binomial for overdispersion; random
   effects for clustering; zero-inflation only if zeros are still short.};
\node[b, below=of f] (g)
  {\textbf{7. Check the final model.} Quantile residual Q--Q, rootogram,
   simulation-based checks, influence.};
\node[b, below=of g, fill=greenlt, draw=greenbox] (h)
  {\textbf{8. Report on the response scale.} Rate ratios with confidence intervals,
   what you checked, what you found, what you did about it.};
\foreach \x/\y in {a/b, b/c, c/d, d/e, e/f, f/g, g/h} \draw[ar] (\x) -- (\y);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
